## Overview
Grocery Dive reviewed its 2025 predictions and graded how accurately they forecasted industry trends, assessing outcomes in private label growth, consumer-facing AI adoption, tariff impacts, health/wellness focus, and other sectors.

## Prediction grades and findings

### 1. Traditional grocers need sharper differentiation (Grade: B)
Traditional supermarket operators took steps to differentiate themselves but "still have a long way to go" in securing their futures. Albertsons and Kroger "recalibrat[ed] their strategies" during 2025, closing stores and cutting costs. Kroger notably "sharply scaled back its automated fulfillment network" late in the year. Ahold Delhaize shifted toward in-store e-commerce fulfillment and continued revitalizing Stop & Shop.

Conversely, specialty chains like Sprouts Farmers Market and discounters including Aldi "continued to grow rapidly," while Walmart expanded sales amid affordability concerns. Grocery Outlet "struggled to connect with shoppers" despite focusing on discounts.

### 2. Consumers continued prioritizing value (Grade: A)
Affordability remained consumers' top concern throughout 2025. Stop & Shop "dropped prices on thousands of products in almost every department" at Massachusetts locations. Aldi "cut prices during the summer on almost a quarter of its selection."

Thanksgiving promotions reflected value emphasis, with Lidl and Aldi offering "Turkey Day-themed promotions" positioned as "less expensive than what they offered last year."

Grocery inflation "hit a two-year high of 2.7% in August" before subsiding somewhat in November.

### 3. Data management by grocers (Grade: Inconclusive)
The prediction proved difficult to assess. Grocery Dive "didn't cover any new or changed data management," though this may reflect that grocers "publicly promote their consumer-facing initiatives more than their back-end operational moves."

### 4. Tariff impacts on grocery industry (Grade: A)
Tariffs announced by President Donald Trump "unleashed instability" as predicted, making planning difficult for retailers and heightening shopper cost-of-living concerns.

Nearly 70% of poll respondents via KPMG "expected grocery prices to increase because of tariffs." FMI reported in August that "more than half of surveyed shoppers said tariffs were their top concern" regarding food costs. In November, the Trump administration "announc[ed] that it would exempt hundreds of agricultural products" from earlier tariff announcements.

### 5. Evolving health and wellness trends (Grade: A)
CPGs and grocers focused on products with attributes like "added protein" or "low sugar." The "Make America Healthy Again movement" gained momentum, and several states restricted "junk" food from SNAP eligibility.

Specialty grocers particularly benefited, posting sales increases as consumers sought them for "nutritional expertise." Target announced it would "add more than 2,000 new wellness-related items across all categories" at year start. Kroger made "health and wellness services a prominent part of its app refresh" in fall.

### 6. Private label pressure on CPGs (Grade: A)
Private label growth exceeded expectations. Aldi announced rebranding its entire private label portfolio, launching "its first-ever namesake brand" with Aldi's name on every product.

Natural Grocers reported "stronger net sales" largely due to its "fast-growing private label assortment," consistently introducing new items and "linking the offerings to its loyalty program."

Households earning above $100,000 were "becoming more likely to buy private label groceries." FMI data showed "86% of surveyed retailer and manufacturer executives plan to moderately or significantly increase private brand investments over the next two years."

### 7. Electronic shelf labels mainstream adoption (Grade: B-)
ESL rollout continued but 2025 remained "relatively quiet" compared to prior-year scrutiny. Walmart and Hy-Vee advanced deployment. The Independent Grocers Association facilitated adoption "through a deal with Aperion." Instacart expanded its "Carrot Tag electronic shelf label software to more retailers," including Stewart's Marketplace and Dierbergs Markets.

A University of California Rady School of Management study found "no evidence of ESLs leading to real-time pricing spikes."

### 8. Consumer-facing AI growth (Grade: A)
AI adoption accelerated significantly. In October, OpenAI partnered with Uber, Instacart, Target, and DoorDash to integrate with ChatGPT, enabling conversational digital food aisle exploration. The following week, Walmart announced customers could make purchases within ChatGPT using the new Instacart Checkout feature.

Albertsons released an AI-powered conversational shopping assistant to help shoppers search for and discover products.

PwC found that while AI-driven virtual assistant acceptance was growing, "nearly two-thirds of respondents said at least one safeguard would help them feel comfortable with AI-powered shopping."

## Summary statistics cited
- Grocery inflation: 2.7% (August 2025, two-year high)
- KPMG poll: ~70% expected price increases from tariffs
- FMI August survey: >50% cited tariffs as top food-cost concern
- FMI September report: 86% of executives planned moderate-to-significant private brand investment increases over two years
